# Assurance WARNING PHASE — the one-level severity downgrade (2026-08-23).
#
# User requirement: "warning mode, when mission critical mode in warning
# phase. which normal error and mission critical warning. when set to warning
# on a mode, one level lower error the level warning."
#
# I.e. a profile (mission-critical especially) can be run in a WARNING PHASE:
# every diagnostic that profile would raise drops EXACTLY ONE severity level.
# An error becomes a warning. This is the migration ramp INTO a stricter
# profile: you see what would fail before the build fails.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS IS A MODIFIER, NOT A PROFILE NAME
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `policy_names.spl` states the alias set is FROZEN, so a new spelling such as
# `critical-warn` is out of bounds by construction. But the decisive reason is
# FAILURE DIRECTION, not the freeze:
#
#   * A name/suffix encoding (`SIMPLE_SAFETY_PROFILE=critical:warn`) makes
#     `normalize_profile_name` return "" at every consumer that was not
#     updated. "" resolves to moderate/Advisory. A suffix therefore fails
#     OPEN — an un-updated projection silently enforces LESS than it does
#     today.
#   * A separate knob fails CLOSED — an un-updated consumer simply ignores the
#     modifier and keeps enforcing FULL severity. Never weaker than today.
#
# Mission-critical infrastructure takes the fail-closed encoding.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DOWNGRADE NEVER REACHES A SILENT LEVEL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is a severity transform, NOT a mute switch. Every ladder in the tree
# has a lowest level that still REPORTS, and the downgrade clamps there:
#
#   driver (SafetyPassSeverity)  Deny -> Warn -> Advisory (clamp)
#       Advisory is log-only via SIMPLE_SAFETY_WARN — still reported, so it is
#       a legitimate floor.
#   lint (LintLevel)             Deny -> Warn (clamp)
#       `Allow` is SILENCE, so lint clamps one rung higher than the enum's
#       bottom. Downgrading into Allow is forbidden.
#   interpreter (bool)           true -> false (one step, then clamp)
#       A bool cannot count levels; see the honesty note in the header of
#       `eval_apply_assurance_profile`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ZERO `use` LINES, ZERO MODULE-LEVEL STATE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same constraint, and for the same reason, as its sibling `policy_names.spl`:
# this leaf is imported by 80.driver, 10.frontend, 90.tools and src/app, and
# module-level `Dict`/`var` state in an interpreter-visible module graph is
# what the contamination bug record names as its likely trigger. Every
# function here is a pure function of its arguments; the ONE environment read
# lives in a thin named wrapper so that all real logic stays testable without
# a process-global.

# The dedicated env knob, mirroring `SIMPLE_SAFETY_PROFILE`'s convention:
# orthogonal to the profile name, never encoded inside it.
pub fn warning_phase_env_var_name() -> text:
    "SIMPLE_ASSURANCE_WARNING_PHASE"

# The CLI spelling, mirroring `--profile`.
pub fn warning_phase_flag_name() -> text:
    "--assurance-warning-phase"

# Parse the knob's value. Accepted truthy spellings are deliberately narrow
# and fail CLOSED: anything unrecognised (including "" and a typo) means NO
# downgrade, i.e. full configured severity.
# Non-`pub` for the same `primitive_api` reason as the rank helpers above.
fn warning_phase_enabled_for_value(raw: text) -> bool:
    val lower = raw.lower()
    if lower == "1" or lower == "true" or lower == "yes" or lower == "on":
        return true
    # `warn` / `warning` read naturally at the CLI ("run this mode in warning
    # phase") and are accepted for that reason only.
    if lower == "warn" or lower == "warning":
        return true
    false

# Canonical severity ladder shared by the projections, strictness-ascending.
# Rank is an ordinal, not a wire format — nothing serialises these numbers.
#
#   0 advisory (log-only, lowest level that still REPORTS)
#   1 warn     (reported, does not fail the build)
#   2 deny     (fails the build)
# Defined relative to each other so the "exactly one rung apart" invariant is
# in the code rather than in three independent literals. Non-`pub` on purpose:
# these are bare-primitive signatures, and the tree's convention (see
# `20.hir/exhaustiveness/coverage_witness.spl:102`,
# `35.semantics/match_exhaustiveness.spl:51`) is to keep such helpers a plain
# `fn`, which is still importable across modules, rather than trip the
# `primitive_api` rule.
fn severity_rank_deny() -> i64:
    2

fn severity_rank_warn() -> i64:
    severity_rank_deny() - 1

fn severity_rank_advisory() -> i64:
    severity_rank_warn() - 1

# Drop exactly one level, clamping at `floor_rank` so the result is never a
# silent level. The caller supplies its own floor because the ladders differ:
# the driver passes `severity_rank_advisory()` (log-only still reports), lint
# passes `severity_rank_warn()` (its `Allow` rung is silence).
#
# Exactly one level: this is deliberately NOT "set everything to warn". A Deny
# under warning phase becomes Warn; a Warn becomes Advisory (driver) and stays
# Warn (lint, by its floor).
# A rank already at or below the floor is returned UNCHANGED — the clamp never
# raises a severity, it only refuses to lower one past the floor.
fn downgrade_severity_rank(rank: i64, floor_rank: i64) -> i64:
    if rank <= floor_rank:
        return rank
    rank - 1

# Convenience: apply the downgrade only when warning phase is on. Keeps every
# call site a single expression and makes "non-warning mode is unchanged" a
# property of ONE function rather than of N scattered `if`s.
fn phased_severity_rank(rank: i64, floor_rank: i64, warning_phase: bool) -> i64:
    if not warning_phase:
        return rank
    downgrade_severity_rank(rank, floor_rank)
